@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Single-Row Subquery";
}

<div class="container-fluid">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">

        <h1 class="fw-bold">
            Single-Row Subquery
        </h1>

        <p class="text-muted">
            Learn how to use a subquery that returns only one row
            or one value.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         WHAT IS SINGLE-ROW SUBQUERY?
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                What is a Single-Row Subquery?
            </h2>

            <p>
                A <strong>Single-Row Subquery</strong> is a subquery
                that returns only one row or one value as its result.
            </p>

            <p>
                Because the subquery returns a single value, we can
                commonly use comparison operators such as
                <strong>=</strong>, <strong>&gt;</strong>,
                <strong>&lt;</strong>, <strong>&gt;=</strong> and
                <strong>&lt;=</strong>.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         BASIC SYNTAX
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Basic Syntax
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
SELECT column_name
FROM table_name
WHERE column_name = (
    SELECT column_name
    FROM table_name
    WHERE condition
);
            </code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3 mb-0">
                The inner query should return only one value when
                using a single-value comparison operator.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         EXAMPLE TABLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example Table
            </h2>

            <p>
                Consider the following <strong>Employees</strong> table:
            </p>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-primary">

                        <tr>
                            <th>EmployeeId</th>
                            <th>EmployeeName</th>
                            <th>Department</th>
                            <th>Salary</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td>1</td>
                            <td>Rahul</td>
                            <td>IT</td>
                            <td>30000</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>2</td>
                            <td>Priya</td>
                            <td>HR</td>
                            <td>45000</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>3</td>
                            <td>Amit</td>
                            <td>IT</td>
                            <td>50000</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>4</td>
                            <td>Sneha</td>
                            <td>Finance</td>
                            <td>40000</td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         EXAMPLE 1
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example 1: Using = Operator
            </h2>

            <p>
                Suppose we want to find employees who have the same
                salary as Priya.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
SELECT EmployeeName, Salary
FROM Employees
WHERE Salary = (
    SELECT Salary
    FROM Employees
    WHERE EmployeeName = 'Priya'
);
            </code></pre>

            <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
                How it works
            </h5>

            <ol>

                <li>
                    The inner query finds Priya's salary.
                </li>

                <li>
                    The inner query returns one value.
                </li>

                <li>
                    The outer query finds employees whose salary
                    matches that value.
                </li>

            </ol>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         EXAMPLE 2
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example 2: Using &gt; Operator
            </h2>

            <p>
                Find employees whose salary is greater than Rahul's
                salary.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
SELECT EmployeeName, Salary
FROM Employees
WHERE Salary &gt; (
    SELECT Salary
    FROM Employees
    WHERE EmployeeName = 'Rahul'
);
            </code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The inner query returns Rahul's salary. The outer query
                then returns employees whose salary is greater than
                Rahul's salary.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         EXAMPLE 3
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example 3: Using Aggregate Function
            </h2>

            <p>
                Aggregate functions such as <strong>MAX()</strong>,
                <strong>MIN()</strong> and <strong>AVG()</strong>
                normally return a single value, making them useful
                with single-row subqueries.
            </p>

            <p>
                Find employees whose salary is equal to the highest
                salary in the table:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded"><code>
SELECT EmployeeName, Salary
FROM Employees
WHERE Salary = (
    SELECT MAX(Salary)
    FROM Employees
);
            </code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The inner query returns the highest salary.
                The outer query finds the employee having that salary.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         COMPARISON OPERATORS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Comparison Operators
            </h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-primary">

                        <tr>
                            <th>Operator</th>
                            <th>Meaning</th>
                            <th>Example</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td>=</td>
                            <td>Equal to</td>
                            <td>Salary = (Subquery)</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>&gt;</td>
                            <td>Greater than</td>
                            <td>Salary &gt; (Subquery)</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>&lt;</td>
                            <td>Less than</td>
                            <td>Salary &lt; (Subquery)</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>&gt;=</td>
                            <td>Greater than or equal to</td>
                            <td>Salary &gt;= (Subquery)</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>&lt;=</td>
                            <td>Less than or equal to</td>
                            <td>Salary &lt;= (Subquery)</td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-warning mb-4">

        <h4 class="fw-bold">
            Important Points
        </h4>

        <ul class="mb-0">

            <li>
                A single-row subquery returns one row or one value.
            </li>

            <li>
                It can commonly be used with comparison operators.
            </li>

            <li>
                The inner query is normally enclosed in parentheses.
            </li>

            <li>
                Aggregate functions such as MAX(), MIN() and AVG()
                can return a single value.
            </li>

            <li>
                If a subquery returns multiple rows while using a
                single-value operator such as =, SQL Server can
                generate an error.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-info p-4 mb-4">

        <h2 class="fw-bold">
            Summary
        </h2>

        <p class="mb-0">
            A Single-Row Subquery returns one row or one value and is
            commonly used with comparison operators such as =, &gt;,
            &lt;, &gt;= and &lt;=. It is useful when the result of one
            query is required for comparison in another query.
        </p>

    </div>


